1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,073
Exam season arrives every year, and it takes skaters by surprise every year.

2
00:00:05,073 --> 00:00:07,542
One week you are training five times.

3
00:00:07,542 --> 00:00:16,220
The next, you have four papers, a revision timetable, and a parent who would quite like you to stay off the road until it is over.

4
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:22,962
So how do you keep your skating without failing your exams, or losing the speed you built all season?

5
00:00:22,962 --> 00:00:25,899
The answer is not doing both at full volume.

6
00:00:25,899 --> 00:00:31,906
It is a deliberately small plan that protects what fades fastest, and lets go of the rest.

7
00:00:32,606 --> 00:00:36,887
Start with what actually happens when training stops for a month.

8
00:00:36,887 --> 00:00:38,995
Your skating is not one quality.

9
00:00:38,995 --> 00:00:42,486
It is several, and they fade at very different rates.

10
00:00:42,486 --> 00:00:44,462
Technique is the most durable.

11
00:00:44,462 --> 00:00:51,839
The feel of a good push, an edge, the timing of a crossover, all of that stays in your nervous system for weeks.

12
00:00:51,839 --> 00:00:57,043
Strength and top speed are stubborn too, as long as they get a little stimulus.

13
00:00:57,043 --> 00:00:59,809
A few short, hard efforts keep them close.

14
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:02,246
Aerobic fitness is the one that goes.

15
00:01:02,246 --> 00:01:09,689
The ability to hold a hard pace for twenty minutes is built by volume, and it leaves first when the volume stops.

16
00:01:09,689 --> 00:01:14,299
The good news is that it is also the quickest of the three to rebuild.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,422
If you take one idea from this video, take this one.

18
00:01:18,422 --> 00:01:22,568
When time collapses, protect how often you train, not how long.

19
00:01:22,568 --> 00:01:31,913
Two thirty minute sessions in a week will do far more for you than one ninety minute session, even though the long one has more skating in it.

20
00:01:31,913 --> 00:01:37,047
Frequency keeps the movement familiar and keeps your legs used to being asked.

21
00:01:37,047 --> 00:01:43,826
A long session also costs you more on the day, and during exams that means a worse evening of revision.

22
00:01:43,826 --> 00:01:46,261
Remember, an exam block is temporary.

23
00:01:46,261 --> 00:01:48,432
You are not trying to get faster.

24
00:01:48,432 --> 00:01:54,882
You are trying to come out the other side able to start training again, rather than starting over.

25
00:01:55,582 --> 00:01:58,565
Here is the shape that works for most skaters.

26
00:01:58,565 --> 00:02:01,613
Two skate sessions a week, thirty minutes each.

27
00:02:01,613 --> 00:02:05,049
One with short sprints, one with easy technique work.

28
00:02:05,049 --> 00:02:08,486
Put them on days with no paper the following morning.

29
00:02:08,486 --> 00:02:18,148
Add one fifteen minute strength session at home, and ten minutes of mobility most days, which is really for your back and hips after hours at a desk.

30
00:02:18,148 --> 00:02:23,400
In the week of your heaviest papers, drop to one skate session plus the mobility.

31
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,827
That is not a failure.

32
00:02:24,827 --> 00:02:26,448
That is the plan working.

33
00:02:26,448 --> 00:02:33,192
Altogether it is about ninety minutes of training a week, and it is enough to hold most of what matters.

34
00:02:33,891 --> 00:02:36,891
So what goes into that thirty minute session?

35
00:02:36,891 --> 00:02:43,489
Warm up properly for ten minutes, because a short session is no excuse for going hard on cold legs.

36
00:02:43,489 --> 00:02:52,552
Then do six to eight sprints of around ten seconds each, from a rolling start, with a full easy lap or a couple of minutes between them.

37
00:02:52,552 --> 00:02:55,285
Finish with five minutes of easy skating.

38
00:02:55,285 --> 00:02:57,484
Keep every sprint genuinely fast.

39
00:02:57,484 --> 00:02:59,683
The point is not to finish tired.

40
00:02:59,683 --> 00:03:02,816
It is to remind your body what fast feels like.

41
00:03:02,816 --> 00:03:06,815
And in your very worst weeks, cut it down to twenty minutes.

42
00:03:06,815 --> 00:03:12,546
An eight minute warm up, four sprints with long recoveries, a few easy laps, and home.

43
00:03:13,246 --> 00:03:16,316
For strength, you need no gym and no equipment.

44
00:03:16,316 --> 00:03:21,607
Squats, split squats, single leg balance, glute bridges, calf raises and a plank.

45
00:03:21,607 --> 00:03:26,898
Two or three rounds, done well rather than fast, and stop well short of soreness.

46
00:03:26,898 --> 00:03:29,119
Next, sleep comes before training.

47
00:03:29,119 --> 00:03:33,691
If something has to go on a given day, cut the session, not the sleep.

48
00:03:33,691 --> 00:03:36,238
Sleep is doing two jobs in exam season.

49
00:03:36,238 --> 00:03:41,072
It locks in what you studied, and it is the only recovery you are getting.

50
00:03:41,072 --> 00:03:43,619
The practical answer is to train early.

51
00:03:43,619 --> 00:03:49,106
Dawn sessions are cooler in an Indian summer, and you finish before revision starts.

52
00:03:49,106 --> 00:03:54,397
Finally, all that sitting shortens your hip flexors and stiffens your lower back.

53
00:03:54,397 --> 00:04:00,602
Ten minutes of mobility in a study break is enough to keep your low skating position available.

54
00:04:01,302 --> 00:04:05,584
Some exam blocks leave no room at all, and a few weeks off is fine.

55
00:04:05,584 --> 00:04:11,399
What is not fine is coming back to skates that have gone backwards while you were revising.

56
00:04:11,399 --> 00:04:17,853
Before the gap, clean and oil your bearings so they are not sitting in grit and moisture for a month.

57
00:04:17,853 --> 00:04:22,582
A bottle of Speedslick Pro bearing oil and twenty minutes is all it takes.

58
00:04:22,582 --> 00:04:32,296
Wipe the frames, check the axles, rotate the wheels, and store everything somewhere dry and out of direct sun, not in a hot car boot or a damp cupboard.

59
00:04:32,296 --> 00:04:36,194
When you come back, spin each wheel before the first session.

60
00:04:36,194 --> 00:04:43,287
A rusty bearing will tell you straight away, and it is far better to find out at home than on the first corner.

61
00:04:43,987 --> 00:04:50,876
After the final exam, the temptation is to go and do the session you have been dreaming about for a month.

62
00:04:50,876 --> 00:04:51,785
Please do not.

63
00:04:51,785 --> 00:04:57,894
Treating the first week back as a celebration is the most common way skaters lose a fortnight.

64
00:04:57,894 --> 00:04:59,973
Build up over two weeks instead.

65
00:04:59,973 --> 00:05:12,385
In week one, do three easy to moderate sessions and nothing maximal, and let your feet get used to the boots again, because skin that has been out of a speed boot for a month blisters easily.

66
00:05:12,385 --> 00:05:16,674
In week two, bring back one hard session and keep the rest steady.

67
00:05:16,674 --> 00:05:19,338
By week three, you are training normally.

68
00:05:20,038 --> 00:05:21,464
A few things to avoid.

69
00:05:21,464 --> 00:05:29,887
Do not try to hold your normal training load, because something will give, and it is usually the exams, your sleep, or your knees.

70
00:05:29,887 --> 00:05:33,451
Do not train late at night to make up a missed session.

71
00:05:33,451 --> 00:05:36,885
Do not test yourself with a time trial on tired legs.

72
00:05:36,885 --> 00:05:39,931
It tells you nothing except that you are tired.

73
00:05:39,931 --> 00:05:43,624
And do not change anything on your skates during the gap.

74
00:05:43,624 --> 00:05:51,399
Save new wheels or a new frame for the comeback, so that if something feels odd, you know whether it is you or the gear.

75
00:05:52,099 --> 00:05:53,566
So, the short version.

76
00:05:53,566 --> 00:05:55,566
Protect frequency, not volume.

77
00:05:55,566 --> 00:06:02,965
Two short sessions and fifteen minutes of strength a week will hold most of your skating through an exam block.

78
00:06:02,965 --> 00:06:12,164
Train early, never trade sleep for a session, look after your bearings if you have to stop, and ramp back over two weeks, not one evening.

79
00:06:12,164 --> 00:06:18,430
The full guide, with the weekly plan and answers to common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

80
00:06:18,430 --> 00:06:20,496
The link is in the description.

81
00:06:20,496 --> 00:06:24,762
Good luck with your exams, and we will see you back on the road.
